# Git commits

Do **not** run `git commit` unless the user explicitly asks you to commit (e.g. they say “commit”, “git commit”, or clearly instruct you to create a commit).

It is fine to make file edits, run tests, and use read-only git commands (`git status`, `git diff`, `git log`, etc.). Only create commits when the user has said to.

If the user wants changes saved to git, they will say so; do not assume a commit is desired after finishing a task.

See also **`AGENTS.md`** at the repository root for the same policy and other agent-oriented notes.
